  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q16-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "16",
    "prompt": "Let $p$ and $q$ be prime numbers. Determine whether the equation $px^2-qx+q=0$ can have rational solutions.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "No rational solutions.\n\nThe Rational Root Theorem restricts possible roots to $\\pm1,\\pm q,\\pm\\frac1p,\\pm\\frac qp$; none satisfies the equation.",
      "worked": "Let $P(x)=px^2-qx+q$. By the Rational Root Theorem, any rational root belongs to $\\left\\{\\pm1,\\pm q,\\pm\\frac1p,\\pm\\frac qp\\right\\}$.\n\nSince $p,q\\ge2$, the positive candidates give\n$$\\begin{align*}P(1)&=p>0,\\\\P(q)&=q\\bigl((p-1)q+1\\bigr)>0,\\\\P\\left(\\frac1p\\right)&=\\frac{1+(p-1)q}{p}>0,\\\\P\\left(\\frac qp\\right)&=q>0.\\end{align*}$$\nFor every $t>0$, $P(-t)=pt^2+qt+q>0$, so none of the negative candidates is a root either. Therefore, the equation has no rational solutions.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 70
  },
